unions are having a moment in the  current labor shortage after decades of  seeing both their influence and power  erode workers now have the upper hand or  at least a more solid footing when it  comes to negotiating pay and benefits  from starbucks coffee shops and apple  stores to an amazon warehouse tens of  thousands of workers across the services  industries are banding together to press  their advantage and this is just the  beginning according to venture capital  investor roy bahad company owners and  senior management need to start paying  attention he says  the business skill of the future is the  ability to lead an organized workforce  so to find out how to get there i asked  for tips from sarah nelson she heads up  the world's largest flight attendant  union what is your advice to corporate  leaders on how to  work effectively with them i i think  what's really important is that people  understand that this is a chance to have  a structure in the workplace and  actually be able to have a pipeline to  the employees and feedback and um to  have a real collaborative relationship  there that's very organized uh that you  don't have to try to make sense of  yourself  as  an executive and and so it can be a real  benefit to uh  to companies who understand how to work  with unions what do senior leaders in  management currently get wrong about  unions and how to work with unions  i think the biggest thing that they get  wrong is just the idea that they need to  fight them  this is a very very  strong narrative in wall street among  the investor world fight the unions keep  the unions out because you want to  retain control there is a strong sense  in this country right now  that people want to have unions people  want to have a voice they want to have  certainty in their workplace and so  fighting that is really a waste of money  and it's it's also it's also setting you  up as bad guy how do you build trust  with  union leaders or union reps so that  there isn't a cynicism or suspicion on  the other side so the biggest thing is  communication when there's going to be a  change in the business get a hold of the  union leaders first schedule a meeting  or  as you build that relationship sometimes  it can just be a phone call  just to advise them about what's coming  next and and get a little feedback from  them and so don't take them off guard  because when you're announcing things in  the workplace that nobody's heard about  um that  builds suspicion and distrust  as opposed to what can happen when you  are being collaborative bringing people  in using that structure of the union so  that the union leaders can say yes we  talked about this and when they're  getting questions from the employees and  actually that takes a lot of stress off  of the admin  of the company  why should ceos not be afraid of unions  particularly when there's always the  possibility of a strike everybody likes  to talk about the strike but really what  the strike is is calling the question um  so it's getting to a solution everyone  has something at risk and a strike and i  always say that a strike has to be a  part of a productive collective  bargaining relationship  because  the workers are putting something on the  line they have to really decide that the  issues that they're disagreeing with  management on are important enough for  them to risk not getting a paycheck and  going without working um and management  then has to uh recognize the risk of the  strike and so usually what that does is  it encourages people to compromise and  come to an agreement
 

